# Rick Dangerous 2 (Micro Style 1990): the five level blocks, 21 760 bytes each at $A800-$FCFF, as the game plays
# them (the maps' last 768 bytes deciphered). One file for src/levels/level1.json .. level5.json: the lines before the
# first @piece are common to every level (the tables at the same address in all five), then each level's own lines
# (the maps, the section table and its lists, the trap kinds; level 5's own code).
#
# The layout of a block (addresses per level in work/stage2/ann/07-notes.md):
#   $A800 CellFlags 256, $A900 CellGraphics 2048, $B100 CellAttrs 256, $B200 Blocks 4096,
#   $C200 LevelHeader (the kind table, the section table, the maps), $C206 the level 5 hooks (text on levels 1-4),
#   $C20C LevelNumber, $C20E SpriteFrameMap 95, $C26D Sprites (63-68 of 128 bytes) up to the maps,
#   Maps, SectionTable and its exit and entry lists, KindTable, TrapAnims, TrapPaths, KindRecords, then zeros
#   (level 5: its code and the Fat Guy's tables from $F7E6).

@module cells ; The cells and the blocks: flags, pixels, attributes, 4 x 4 blocks
@about A level is drawn from 256 cells of 8 x 8 pixels, each with a flag byte (what Rick and the objects meet there) and an attribute, and from 256 blocks of 4 x 4 cells; the sections' maps are rows of block numbers. All four tables are full size in every level; the editable form is one image of the cells and one of the blocks (the lead's converters).

$A800 b CellFlags 8 ; A flag byte per cell: solid, floor, ladder, deadly, in front of the sprites
> One byte per cell number 0-255. Bit 0 a special surface (feet row only; level 1 a weak jump, 2 ice, 3 a bounce, 4 mud, 5 a launch pad: $7EF1 decides), bit 1 solid, bit 2 a floor one can jump up through (feet row only), bit 3 a ladder, bit 4 a ladder's top (down starts a climb), bit 5 deadly (Rick dies, a detonator blows), bit 6 not used in the cells (the body test sets it for a platform object), bit 7 the cell is drawn in front of the sprites: the composer leaves it (ComposeCell $7755).
> Read by the body tests $8FF9 / $8F29 (through ViewCells), ProbePoint $8D0C (the bullet), ProbeGround $8E1C (the detonator), the enemies' probes and the composer.
@res level/cell-flags.bin

$A900 b CellGraphics 8 ; The pixels of the 256 cells, 8 bytes each
> Cell n at CellGraphics + 8n: 8 bytes, the top pixel row first, bit 7 the left pixel. Drawn by the cell drawers through the base operands at $772F and $7776 (the text screens point them at the font $7080 for a while).
@res level/cell-graphics.bin

$B100 b CellAttrs 8 ; A screen attribute per cell
> Cell n's attribute (FLASH, BRIGHT, PAPER, INK) at CellAttrs + n, written by SetViewAttrs $783F and the scroll for every view cell drawn; the sprites never change attributes.
@res level/cell-attrs.bin

$B200 b Blocks 16 ; 256 blocks of 4 x 4 cell numbers
> Block n at Blocks + 16n: four rows of four cell numbers, the top row first. BuildView $88D4 copies a block as a 4 x 4 square into ViewCells (POP DE, two cells at a time). A map byte is a block number.
@res level/blocks.bin

@module header ; The level header, the level number and the sprite frame map
@about Six bytes at $C200 tell the game where this level's tables are; the level number says which level the block is (LoadLevel reads blocks off side B until it matches); the frame map turns the animation scripts' frame numbers into sprite numbers.

$C200 w LevelHeader 1 ; The addresses of this level's kind table, section table and maps
> Three words. +0 KindTable (read by SpawnMapObject $9D6C), +2 SectionTable (SetupSection $8A6D, CheckSectionExit's setup, ResetSectionObjects $8379), +4 Maps, the base the section table's map offsets are added to (SetupSection, and DecryptMaps $894E: the 768 bytes from Maps + $600 come enciphered on side B).
$C202 ; +2: the section table
$C204 ; +4: the maps' base

$C20C b LevelNumber 1 ; The level this block holds, 1-5
> LevelStart $7CC5 compares it with Level $7EF1: equal, the level is in memory; otherwise LoadLevel $840C reads the blocks of side B until this byte says the wanted level. ResetGame $7CAD reads it too.
$C20D ; 0 in every level: the place of frame 0 (SpriteFrameMap - 1 + frame; scripts number frames from 1)

$C20E b SpriteFrameMap 8 ; A sprite number per animation frame 1-95: bit 7 mirrored, $7F none
> AnimateTrap $A608 reads the byte at SpriteFrameMap - 1 + frame for a frame of a trap's animation script: bits 0-6 the sprite (Sprites + 128n), bit 7 drawn mirrored (record +6 bit 7), $7F no sprite (the record's sprite word 0). Frames past the level's last (86-89 used) are 0. Frames 1-12 are sprites 1-12, 13-25 sprites 0-12 mirrored (Rick's), then the level's own sprites, several mirrored; frames 1-60 are the same in all five levels.
@res level/frame-map.bin
$C21A ; frames 13-25: sprites 0-12 mirrored
$C227 ; frames 26-: sprites 13 on (frame 30 and 31 both sprite 17)

$C26D b Sprites 8 ; The level's sprites, 128 bytes each, up to the maps
> Sprite n at Sprites + 128n (63 to 68 sprites: as far as the maps). Byte 0 a line offset added to the drawing row, byte 1 the number of rows (at most 21), then 21 rows of 6 bytes: data 2, data 1, data 0, mask 0, mask 2, mask 1 (data 0 the left byte; ComposeSprite $7329 pops them as HL, BC, DE). 24 pixels wide, 21 high; the mask is ANDed, then the data ORed; mirrored sprites go through MirrorTable $6500.
> Sprites 0-26 are the same bytes in every level (Rick, his shots, the blast, the ammunition and detonator boxes); 27 on are the level's own.
@res level/sprites.bin
$C26D ; sprite 0: Rick standing; 1 punch ready; 2-5 walking; 6-7 crawling; 8 jumping or falling; 9 shooting; 10 punching; 11 throwing; 12 riding; 13-14 climbing; 15-16 dying
$CAED ; sprite 17: the bullet; 18-19 the detonator (19 its flash); 20-24 the blast
$CEED ; sprite 25: the ammunition box; 26 the detonator box
$CFED ; sprite 27: the treasure (the first sprite that differs between levels)
$D06D ; sprites 28-30: an enemy walking; 31 dying; 32-33 climbing; 34-39 the second set (enemy kind bit 2)

@piece level1

@module header
$C206 t LevelHookA ; "CORE  ": text where level 5 has its two jumps (never run on this level)
> Six bytes of text, "CORE  " (levels 1 and 3 say "CORE  ", levels 2 and 4 "DESIGN": Core Design, the developers). Level 5 holds JP $F7E6 and JP $F8D0 here; on this level LevelHookOn $8A24 is never set, so SetupSection ($8AF8) and UpdateObjects ($8664) never call $C206 / $C209.
$C209 LevelHookB ; the second jump's place

@module maps ; The sections' maps: rows of 8 block numbers
$E46D b Maps 8 ; The maps of the 8 sections, one row of 8 block numbers a line
> LevelHeader+4 points here. A section's map is a column of rows, 8 block numbers a row (8 x 4 cells = 256 pixels: one screen wide), the top row first; the section table's +0/+1 (big-endian) is the offset of its first row from here. No height is stored: BuildView $88D4 reads 11 rows from row ViewTop / 4 into ViewCells (44 cell rows), the screen shows view rows 9-31 under the HUD, and the scroll limit (section table +3) is the last ViewTop. So a section shows block rows 2 to limit / 4 + 7 (rows 0-1 lie above the screen, rows limit / 4 + 8 to + 10 are built below it for the objects there).
> The maps follow each other in memory (in section order but for the section-table filler entries). A map often starts 2 rows before the end of the previous one's shown rows: those 2 rows are the next section's hidden rows 0-1 (a packing that costs nothing). The comment on each section gives its rows to the next map and what the limit asks for.
@res level/maps.bin
$E46D ; section 0: scroll limit 0, 8 rows to the next map, rows 2-7 shown; also the map of the filler entries 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16
$E4AD Section1Map ; section 1: scroll limit 112, 30 rows to the next map, rows 2-35 shown: rows 30-35 are section 2's rows 0-5
$E59D Section2Map ; section 2: scroll limit 112, 40 rows to the next map, rows 2-35 shown; rows 36-38 only built below the view; row 39 read by nothing (filler)
$E6DD Section3Map ; section 3: scroll limit 0, 8 rows to the next map, rows 2-7 shown
$E71D Section4Map ; section 4: scroll limit 104, 32 rows to the next map, rows 2-33 shown: rows 32-33 are section 5's rows 0-1
$E81D Section5Map ; section 5: scroll limit 104, 32 rows to the next map, rows 2-33 shown: rows 32-33 are section 6's rows 0-1
$E91D Section6Map ; section 6: scroll limit 8, 8 rows to the next map, rows 2-9 shown: rows 8-9 are section 7's rows 0-1
$E95D Section7Map ; section 7: scroll limit 8, 8 rows shown up to its row 9
$E9AD s MapsTail1 ; 185 zero bytes after the maps
> Zero bytes up to the next block. BuildView reads the first 24 bytes as the 3 rows it builds below the view of the section whose shown rows end at $E9AD (scrolled to its limit); no section shows them.
$EA66 b MapsTail2 8 ; 6 bytes after the maps
> Bytes no section shows (01 01 01 01 01 01).
$EA6C s MapsTail3 ; 769 zero bytes after the maps
> Zero bytes up to the next block.

@module sections ; The section table, the exit lists and the map entry lists
$ED6D b SectionTable 8 ; 17 entries of 8 bytes: 8 sections and 9 filler entries
> LevelHeader+2 points here; SetupSection $8A6D reads entry A at SectionTable + 8A. +0/+1 the map's offset from Maps (big-endian); +2 0, read by no code; +3 the scroll limit, the last ViewTop (cells, a multiple of 8) the scroll down may reach ($88D2); +4/+5 the exit list's offset from SectionTable (big-endian), into CheckSectionExit's LD IX,n; +6/+7 the map entry list's offset from SectionTable (big-endian), into SectionEntries $8B3E. The number of entries is not stored: SectionCounts $8374 says 9 for this level (ResetSectionObjects $8379 walks that many lists).
> The filler entries (8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16) have map offset 0 (section 0's map), limit 0 and empty lists (two zero bytes each; the last entry's lists are the 0 word KindTable starts with). Only the first of them is inside SectionCounts (section 8); the lead's recording sec-l1-s8 set it up: an empty screen of section 0's map.
@res level/sections.bin
$ED6D ; section 0: map +$0000, limit 0, exits +$0088, entries +$008D
$ED75 ; section 1: map +$0040, limit 112, exits +$00C2, entries +$00CB
$ED7D ; section 2: map +$0130, limit 112, exits +$0198, entries +$01B1
$ED85 ; section 3: map +$0270, limit 0, exits +$026E, entries +$0273
$ED8D ; section 4: map +$02B0, limit 104, exits +$028C, entries +$0299
$ED95 ; section 5: map +$03B0, limit 104, exits +$036A, entries +$0377
$ED9D ; section 6: map +$04B0, limit 8, exits +$043C, entries +$0441
$EDA5 ; section 7: map +$04F0, limit 8, exits +$0462, entries +$0467
$EDAD ; filler entries 8-16
$EDF5 b Section0Exits 4 ; Section 0's exits: 1, then the 0 end
> 4 bytes an exit: +0 flags (bits 0-1 the side Rick leaves the view at: 1 left, 2 right; bit 4 ends the level even with CheatExits; bit 5 RideMode on the way in; bit 6 LevelHookOn = 20; bit 7 the level ends), +1 the section row of Rick's feet (ViewTop + (y + 20) / 8) at which it is taken, +2 the section entered, +3 the row of that section his feet arrive at (the new ViewTop = +3 - his feet row in the view). A byte with bits 0, 1, 3 clear ends the list (a single 0). CheckSectionExit $8993 reads them.
@res level/sections.bin
$EDF5 ; flags $02: leaves at the right at row $1E -> section 2, row $8E
$EDF9 ; the end
$EDFA b Section0Objects 4 ; Section 0's map entries: 6, then the 0 end
> The map entries, sorted by row: 4 bytes each, then +3 bits 0-1 trigger boxes of 4 bytes. +0 the kind (bit 7 set while its object lives: ResetSectionObjects $8379 and FreeObject $9C57 clear it): below $74 a trap or pickup through KindTable, $78 the countdown bonus starts, $7C it stops and pays, any other value from $74 an enemy (bits 0-1 its behaviour: 0 and 3 wander, 1 patrols, 2 chases, 3 also climbs after Rick; bit 2 the second sprite set). +1 the cell row in the section. +2 bits 0-4 the column (x = column x 8), bit 5 x + 4 pixels, bit 6 y + 3 pixels (traps), bit 7 the spawn rule (set: when its row comes into the view; clear: when one of its boxes fires). +3 bits 0-1 the boxes, bits 2-5 a trap's behaviour ($20 treasure, $28 ammunition, $2C detonators: pickups) or an enemy's patrol length ((n + 1) x 8 pixels), bit 6 one-shot (never re-armed). A kind 0 ends the list.
> A trigger box: +0 x in pixels, +1 the cell row, +2 the size (high nibble rows - 1, low nibble columns - 1), +3 who sets it off (bit 0 Rick, 1 the bullet, 2 the blast, 3 the punch, 4 an enemy, 5 another trap; bit 6 fires on every pass; bit 7 the latch TestTriggerBoxes $A69F keeps). ScanMapObjects $8B42 and SpawnFromMap $9C97 read the entries, TestTriggerBoxes the boxes.
@res level/sections.bin
$EDFA ; kind $0D: trap, behaviour $10; row $0F, column 17; spawns on its box, 1 box
$EDFE ; trigger box: x 96, row $1E, 3 x 1 cells, set off by trap
$EE02 ; kind $0D: trap, behaviour $10; row $0F, column 21; spawns on its box, 1 box
$EE06 ; trigger box: x 128, row $1E, 3 x 1 cells, set off by trap
$EE0A ; kind $0D: trap, behaviour $10; row $0F, column 25; spawns on its box, 1 box
$EE0E ; trigger box: x 160, row $1E, 3 x 1 cells, set off by trap
$EE12 ; kind $0E: trap, behaviour $10; row $0F, column 5; spawns on its box, 1 box
$EE16 ; trigger box: x 168, row $19, 8 x 6 cells, set off by Rick
$EE1A ; kind $0D: trap, behaviour $10; row $0F, column 13; spawns on its box, 1 box
$EE1E ; trigger box: x 64, row $1E, 3 x 1 cells, set off by trap
$EE22 ; kind $0D: trap, behaviour $10; row $0F, column 9; spawns on its box, 2 boxes
$EE26 ; trigger box: x 96, row $18, 1 x 7 cells, set off by Rick
$EE2A ; trigger box: x 192, row $1E, 3 x 1 cells, set off by trap
$EE2E ; the end
$EE2F b Section1Exits 4 ; Section 1's exits: 2, then the 0 end
@res level/sections.bin
$EE2F ; flags $92: leaves at the right at row $17 -> section 0, row $17; bit 7 + bit 4: ends the level (also with CheatExits)
$EE33 ; flags $01: leaves at the left at row $83 -> section 4, row $1B
$EE37 ; the end
$EE38 b Section1Objects 4 ; Section 1's map entries: 30, then the 0 end
@res level/sections.bin
$EE38 ; kind $30: trap, behaviour $00; row $11, column 21 +4 px; spawns on its box, 1 box
$EE3C ; trigger box: x 32, row $18, 8 x 1 cells, set off by Rick
$EE40 ; kind $7C: the countdown bonus stops and pays; row $15, column 28; spawns on its box, one-shot, 1 box
$EE44 ; trigger box: x 224, row $14, 4 x 4 cells, set off by Rick
$EE48 ; kind $7B: enemy wanders and climbs after Rick (sprite set 0), patrol 8 px; row $1D, column 4; spawns in view
$EE4C ; kind $2D: trap, behaviour $10; row $1D, column 3; spawns on its box, 2 boxes
$EE50 ; trigger box: x 64, row $1C, 4 x 1 cells, set off by Rick/enemy
$EE54 ; trigger box: x 224, row $1E, 1 x 2 cells, set off by punch
$EE58 ; kind $2C: trap, behaviour $14; row $21, column 26; spawns in view, 1 box
$EE5C ; trigger box: x 24, row $26, 1 x 2 cells, set off by punch
$EE60 ; kind $7D: enemy patrols (sprite set 1), patrol 24 px; row $25, column 16; spawns in view
$EE64 ; kind $77: enemy wanders and climbs after Rick (sprite set 1), patrol 8 px; row $29, column 25; spawns in view
$EE68 ; kind $2B: trap, behaviour $24; row $2C, column 4 +4 px; spawns in view, 1 box
$EE6C ; trigger box: x 24, row $2A, 1 x 2 cells, set off by punch
$EE70 ; kind $0B: trap, behaviour $24; row $34, column 8 +4 px; spawns in view, 1 box
$EE74 ; trigger box: x 224, row $32, 1 x 2 cells, set off by punch
$EE78 ; kind $1E: trap, behaviour $10; row $39, column 26; spawns on its box, 1 box
$EE7C ; trigger box: x 112, row $39, 3 x 3 cells, set off by Rick, every pass
$EE80 ; kind $15: ammunition pickup; row $3D, column 21, y +3 px; spawns in view, 1 box
$EE84 ; trigger box: x 168, row $3D, 3 x 3 cells, set off by Rick
$EE88 ; kind $78: the countdown bonus starts; row $45, column 4; spawns on its box, 1 box
$EE8C ; trigger box: x 32, row $42, 4 x 6 cells, set off by Rick
$EE90 ; kind $2A: trap, behaviour $00; row $46, column 9; spawns in view
$EE94 ; kind $2A: trap, behaviour $00; row $46, column 12; spawns in view
$EE98 ; kind $7D: enemy patrols (sprite set 1), patrol 24 px; row $4D, column 8; spawns in view
$EE9C ; kind $1E: trap, behaviour $10; row $51, column 26; spawns on its box, 2 boxes
