> Read by no code.

@piece level2

@module header
$C206 t LevelHookA ; "DESIGN": text where level 5 has its two jumps (never run on this level)
> Six bytes of text, "DESIGN" (levels 1 and 3 say "CORE  ", levels 2 and 4 "DESIGN": Core Design, the developers). Level 5 holds JP $F7E6 and JP $F8D0 here; on this level LevelHookOn $8A24 is never set, so SetupSection ($8AF8) and UpdateObjects ($8664) never call $C206 / $C209.
$C209 LevelHookB ; the second jump's place

@module maps ; The sections' maps: rows of 8 block numbers
$E46D b Maps 8 ; The maps of the 13 sections, one row of 8 block numbers a line
> LevelHeader+4 points here. A section's map is a column of rows, 8 block numbers a row (8 x 4 cells = 256 pixels: one screen wide), the top row first; the section table's +0/+1 (big-endian) is the offset of its first row from here. No height is stored: BuildView $88D4 reads 11 rows from row ViewTop / 4 into ViewCells (44 cell rows), the screen shows view rows 9-31 under the HUD, and the scroll limit (section table +3) is the last ViewTop. So a section shows block rows 2 to limit / 4 + 7 (rows 0-1 lie above the screen, rows limit / 4 + 8 to + 10 are built below it for the objects there).
> The maps follow each other in memory (in section order but for the section-table filler entries). A map often starts 2 rows before the end of the previous one's shown rows: those 2 rows are the next section's hidden rows 0-1 (a packing that costs nothing). The comment on each section gives its rows to the next map and what the limit asks for.
@res level/maps.bin
$E46D ; section 0: scroll limit 0, 8 rows to the next map, rows 2-7 shown; also the map of the filler entries 13
$E4AD Section1Map ; section 1: scroll limit 144, 42 rows to the next map, rows 2-43 shown: rows 42-43 are section 2's rows 0-1
$E5FD Section2Map ; section 2: scroll limit 16, 10 rows to the next map, rows 2-11 shown: rows 10-11 are section 3's rows 0-1
$E64D Section3Map ; section 3: scroll limit 72, 20 rows to the next map, rows 2-25 shown: rows 20-25 are section 4's rows 0-5
$E6ED Section4Map ; section 4: scroll limit 112, 30 rows to the next map, rows 2-35 shown: rows 30-35 are section 5's rows 0-5
$E7DD Section5Map ; section 5: scroll limit 16, 8 rows to the next map, rows 2-11 shown: rows 8-11 are section 6's rows 0-3
$E81D Section6Map ; section 6: scroll limit 88, 28 rows to the next map, rows 2-29 shown: rows 28-29 are section 7's rows 0-1
$E8FD Section7Map ; section 7: scroll limit 8, 8 rows to the next map, rows 2-9 shown: rows 8-9 are section 8's rows 0-1
$E93D Section8Map ; section 8: scroll limit 8, 8 rows to the next map, rows 2-9 shown: rows 8-9 are section 9's rows 0-1
$E97D Section9Map ; section 9: scroll limit 8, 30 rows to the next map, rows 2-9 shown; rows 10-12 only built below the view; rows 13-29 read by nothing (filler)
$EA6D Section10Map ; section 10: scroll limit 128, 38 rows to the next map, rows 2-39 shown: rows 38-39 are section 11's rows 0-1
$EB9D Section11Map ; section 11: scroll limit 96, 32 rows to the next map, rows 2-31 shown
$EC9D Section12Map ; section 12: scroll limit 8, 8 rows shown up to its row 9
$ECED s MapsTail ; 128 zero bytes after the maps
> Zero bytes up to the next block. BuildView reads the first 24 bytes as the 3 rows it builds below the view of the section whose shown rows end at $ECED (scrolled to its limit); no section shows them.

@module sections ; The section table, the exit lists and the map entry lists
$ED6D b SectionTable 8 ; 14 entries of 8 bytes: 13 sections and 1 filler entries
> LevelHeader+2 points here; SetupSection $8A6D reads entry A at SectionTable + 8A. +0/+1 the map's offset from Maps (big-endian); +2 0, read by no code; +3 the scroll limit, the last ViewTop (cells, a multiple of 8) the scroll down may reach ($88D2); +4/+5 the exit list's offset from SectionTable (big-endian), into CheckSectionExit's LD IX,n; +6/+7 the map entry list's offset from SectionTable (big-endian), into SectionEntries $8B3E. The number of entries is not stored: SectionCounts $8374 says 13 for this level (ResetSectionObjects $8379 walks that many lists).
> The filler entries (13) have map offset 0 (section 0's map), limit 0 and empty lists (two zero bytes each; the last entry's lists are the 0 word KindTable starts with). They lie past SectionCounts: no code reaches them.
@res level/sections.bin
$ED6D ; section 0: map +$0000, limit 0, exits +$0070, entries +$0075
$ED75 ; section 1: map +$0040, limit 144, exits +$0092, entries +$009F
$ED7D ; section 2: map +$0190, limit 16, exits +$01A8, entries +$01B1
$ED85 ; section 3: map +$01E0, limit 72, exits +$01DA, entries +$01E3
$ED8D ; section 4: map +$0280, limit 112, exits +$0254, entries +$0261
$ED95 ; section 5: map +$0370, limit 16, exits +$0316, entries +$0323
$ED9D ; section 6: map +$03B0, limit 88, exits +$034C, entries +$0359
$EDA5 ; section 7: map +$0490, limit 8, exits +$0406, entries +$040F
$EDAD ; section 8: map +$04D0, limit 8, exits +$0434, entries +$0439
$EDB5 ; section 9: map +$0510, limit 8, exits +$045A, entries +$045F
$EDBD ; section 10: map +$0600, limit 128, exits +$0480, entries +$0489
$EDC5 ; section 11: map +$0730, limit 96, exits +$057E, entries +$0587
$EDCD ; section 12: map +$0830, limit 8, exits +$064C, entries +$0655
$EDD5 ; filler entries 13-13
$EDDD b Section0Exits 4 ; Section 0's exits: 1, then the 0 end
> 4 bytes an exit: +0 flags (bits 0-1 the side Rick leaves the view at: 1 left, 2 right; bit 4 ends the level even with CheatExits; bit 5 RideMode on the way in; bit 6 LevelHookOn = 20; bit 7 the level ends), +1 the section row of Rick's feet (ViewTop + (y + 20) / 8) at which it is taken, +2 the section entered, +3 the row of that section his feet arrive at (the new ViewTop = +3 - his feet row in the view). A byte with bits 0, 1, 3 clear ends the list (a single 0). CheckSectionExit $8993 reads them.
@res level/sections.bin
$EDDD ; flags $02: leaves at the right at row $17 -> section 1, row $A7
$EDE1 ; the end
$EDE2 b Section0Objects 4 ; Section 0's map entries: 5, then the 0 end
> The map entries, sorted by row: 4 bytes each, then +3 bits 0-1 trigger boxes of 4 bytes. +0 the kind (bit 7 set while its object lives: ResetSectionObjects $8379 and FreeObject $9C57 clear it): below $74 a trap or pickup through KindTable, $78 the countdown bonus starts, $7C it stops and pays, any other value from $74 an enemy (bits 0-1 its behaviour: 0 and 3 wander, 1 patrols, 2 chases, 3 also climbs after Rick; bit 2 the second sprite set). +1 the cell row in the section. +2 bits 0-4 the column (x = column x 8), bit 5 x + 4 pixels, bit 6 y + 3 pixels (traps), bit 7 the spawn rule (set: when its row comes into the view; clear: when one of its boxes fires). +3 bits 0-1 the boxes, bits 2-5 a trap's behaviour ($20 treasure, $28 ammunition, $2C detonators: pickups) or an enemy's patrol length ((n + 1) x 8 pixels), bit 6 one-shot (never re-armed). A kind 0 ends the list.
> A trigger box: +0 x in pixels, +1 the cell row, +2 the size (high nibble rows - 1, low nibble columns - 1), +3 who sets it off (bit 0 Rick, 1 the bullet, 2 the blast, 3 the punch, 4 an enemy, 5 another trap; bit 6 fires on every pass; bit 7 the latch TestTriggerBoxes $A69F keeps). ScanMapObjects $8B42 and SpawnFromMap $9C97 read the entries, TestTriggerBoxes the boxes.
@res level/sections.bin
$EDE2 ; kind $14: trap, behaviour $18; row $0C, column 13, y +3 px; spawns in view, 1 box
$EDE6 ; trigger box: x 96, row $10, 4 x 12 cells, set off by Rick
$EDEA ; kind $76: enemy chases (sprite set 1), patrol 8 px; row $11, column 16; spawns in view
$EDEE ; kind $7F: enemy wanders and climbs after Rick (sprite set 1), patrol 8 px; row $11, column 4; spawns in view
