$F94A s FreeTail ; 950 zero bytes to the block's end $FCFF
> Read by no code.

@piece level3

@module header
$C206 t LevelHookA ; "CORE  ": text where level 5 has its two jumps (never run on this level)
> Six bytes of text, "CORE  " (levels 1 and 3 say "CORE  ", levels 2 and 4 "DESIGN": Core Design, the developers). Level 5 holds JP $F7E6 and JP $F8D0 here; on this level LevelHookOn $8A24 is never set, so SetupSection ($8AF8) and UpdateObjects ($8664) never call $C206 / $C209.
$C209 LevelHookB ; the second jump's place

@module maps ; The sections' maps: rows of 8 block numbers
$E2ED b Maps 8 ; The maps of the 10 sections, one row of 8 block numbers a line
> LevelHeader+4 points here. A section's map is a column of rows, 8 block numbers a row (8 x 4 cells = 256 pixels: one screen wide), the top row first; the section table's +0/+1 (big-endian) is the offset of its first row from here. No height is stored: BuildView $88D4 reads 11 rows from row ViewTop / 4 into ViewCells (44 cell rows), the screen shows view rows 9-31 under the HUD, and the scroll limit (section table +3) is the last ViewTop. So a section shows block rows 2 to limit / 4 + 7 (rows 0-1 lie above the screen, rows limit / 4 + 8 to + 10 are built below it for the objects there).
> The maps follow each other in memory (in section order but for the section-table filler entries). A map often starts 2 rows before the end of the previous one's shown rows: those 2 rows are the next section's hidden rows 0-1 (a packing that costs nothing). The comment on each section gives its rows to the next map and what the limit asks for.
@res level/maps.bin
$E2ED ; section 0: scroll limit 64, 20 rows to the next map, rows 2-23 shown: rows 20-23 are section 1's rows 0-3; also the map of the filler entries 10, 11, 12, 13
$E38D Section1Map ; section 1: scroll limit 144, 44 rows to the next map, rows 2-43 shown
$E4ED Section2Map ; section 2: scroll limit 96, 32 rows to the next map, rows 2-31 shown
$E5ED Section3Map ; section 3: scroll limit 144, 44 rows to the next map, rows 2-43 shown
$E74D Section4Map ; section 4: scroll limit 8, 8 rows to the next map, rows 2-9 shown: rows 8-9 are section 5's rows 0-1
$E78D Section5Map ; section 5: scroll limit 0, 8 rows to the next map, rows 2-7 shown
$E7CD Section6Map ; section 6: scroll limit 112, 36 rows to the next map, rows 2-35 shown
$E8ED Section7Map ; section 7: scroll limit 8, 9 rows to the next map, rows 2-9 shown: row 9 is section 8's row 0
$E935 Section8Map ; section 8: scroll limit 120, 37 rows to the next map, rows 2-37 shown: row 37 is section 9's row 0
$EA5D Section9Map ; section 9: scroll limit 56, 20 rows shown up to its row 21
$EB0D b MapsTail1 8 ; 8 bytes after the maps
> Bytes no section shows (01 01 01 01 01 01 01 01). BuildView reads the first 24 bytes as the 3 rows it builds below the view of the section whose shown rows end at $EB0D (scrolled to its limit); no section shows them.
$EB15 s MapsTail2 ; 216 zero bytes after the maps
> Zero bytes up to the next block.

@module sections ; The section table, the exit lists and the map entry lists
$EBED b SectionTable 8 ; 14 entries of 8 bytes: 10 sections and 4 filler entries
> LevelHeader+2 points here; SetupSection $8A6D reads entry A at SectionTable + 8A. +0/+1 the map's offset from Maps (big-endian); +2 0, read by no code; +3 the scroll limit, the last ViewTop (cells, a multiple of 8) the scroll down may reach ($88D2); +4/+5 the exit list's offset from SectionTable (big-endian), into CheckSectionExit's LD IX,n; +6/+7 the map entry list's offset from SectionTable (big-endian), into SectionEntries $8B3E. The number of entries is not stored: SectionCounts $8374 says 10 for this level (ResetSectionObjects $8379 walks that many lists).
> The filler entries (10, 11, 12, 13) have map offset 0 (section 0's map), limit 0 and empty lists (two zero bytes each; the last entry's lists are the 0 word KindTable starts with). They lie past SectionCounts: no code reaches them.
@res level/sections.bin
$EBED ; section 0: map +$0000, limit 64, exits +$0070, entries +$0079
$EBF5 ; section 1: map +$00A0, limit 144, exits +$00BE, entries +$00CF
$EBFD ; section 2: map +$0200, limit 96, exits +$01AC, entries +$01C5
$EC05 ; section 3: map +$0300, limit 144, exits +$02A2, entries +$02BB
$EC0D ; section 4: map +$0460, limit 8, exits +$040C, entries +$0415
$EC15 ; section 5: map +$04A0, limit 0, exits +$042E, entries +$0437
$EC1D ; section 6: map +$04E0, limit 112, exits +$0454, entries +$0461
$EC25 ; section 7: map +$0600, limit 8, exits +$0592, entries +$059B
$EC2D ; section 8: map +$0648, limit 120, exits +$05BC, entries +$05C5
$EC35 ; section 9: map +$0770, limit 56, exits +$06A6, entries +$06AB
$EC3D ; filler entries 10-13
$EC5D b Section0Exits 4 ; Section 0's exits: 2, then the 0 end
> 4 bytes an exit: +0 flags (bits 0-1 the side Rick leaves the view at: 1 left, 2 right; bit 4 ends the level even with CheatExits; bit 5 RideMode on the way in; bit 6 LevelHookOn = 20; bit 7 the level ends), +1 the section row of Rick's feet (ViewTop + (y + 20) / 8) at which it is taken, +2 the section entered, +3 the row of that section his feet arrive at (the new ViewTop = +3 - his feet row in the view). A byte with bits 0, 1, 3 clear ends the list (a single 0). CheckSectionExit $8993 reads them.
@res level/sections.bin
$EC5D ; flags $02: leaves at the right at row $17 -> section 1, row $47
$EC61 ; flags $02: leaves at the right at row $47 -> section 1, row $5F
$EC65 ; the end
$EC66 b Section0Objects 4 ; Section 0's map entries: 11, then the 0 end
> The map entries, sorted by row: 4 bytes each, then +3 bits 0-1 trigger boxes of 4 bytes. +0 the kind (bit 7 set while its object lives: ResetSectionObjects $8379 and FreeObject $9C57 clear it): below $74 a trap or pickup through KindTable, $78 the countdown bonus starts, $7C it stops and pays, any other value from $74 an enemy (bits 0-1 its behaviour: 0 and 3 wander, 1 patrols, 2 chases, 3 also climbs after Rick; bit 2 the second sprite set). +1 the cell row in the section. +2 bits 0-4 the column (x = column x 8), bit 5 x + 4 pixels, bit 6 y + 3 pixels (traps), bit 7 the spawn rule (set: when its row comes into the view; clear: when one of its boxes fires). +3 bits 0-1 the boxes, bits 2-5 a trap's behaviour ($20 treasure, $28 ammunition, $2C detonators: pickups) or an enemy's patrol length ((n + 1) x 8 pixels), bit 6 one-shot (never re-armed). A kind 0 ends the list.
> A trigger box: +0 x in pixels, +1 the cell row, +2 the size (high nibble rows - 1, low nibble columns - 1), +3 who sets it off (bit 0 Rick, 1 the bullet, 2 the blast, 3 the punch, 4 an enemy, 5 another trap; bit 6 fires on every pass; bit 7 the latch TestTriggerBoxes $A69F keeps). ScanMapObjects $8B42 and SpawnFromMap $9C97 read the entries, TestTriggerBoxes the boxes.
@res level/sections.bin
$EC66 ; kind $15: trap, behaviour $08; row $15, column 25, y +3 px; spawns in view, 1 box
$EC6A ; trigger box: x 128, row $1A, 4 x 1 cells, set off by Rick
