> Read by no code.

@piece level5

@module maps ; The sections' maps: rows of 8 block numbers
$E3ED b Maps 8 ; The maps of the 8 sections, one row of 8 block numbers a line
> LevelHeader+4 points here. A section's map is a column of rows, 8 block numbers a row (8 x 4 cells = 256 pixels: one screen wide), the top row first; the section table's +0/+1 (big-endian) is the offset of its first row from here. No height is stored: BuildView $88D4 reads 11 rows from row ViewTop / 4 into ViewCells (44 cell rows), the screen shows view rows 9-31 under the HUD, and the scroll limit (section table +3) is the last ViewTop. So a section shows block rows 2 to limit / 4 + 7 (rows 0-1 lie above the screen, rows limit / 4 + 8 to + 10 are built below it for the objects there).
> The maps follow each other in memory (in section order but for the section-table filler entries). A map often starts 2 rows before the end of the previous one's shown rows: those 2 rows are the next section's hidden rows 0-1 (a packing that costs nothing). The comment on each section gives its rows to the next map and what the limit asks for.
@res level/maps.bin
$E3ED ; section 0: scroll limit 104, 34 rows to the next map, rows 2-33 shown
$E4FD Section1Map ; section 1: scroll limit 8, 10 rows to the next map, rows 2-9 shown
$E54D Section2Map ; section 2: scroll limit 80, 28 rows to the next map, rows 2-27 shown
$E62D Section3Map ; section 3: scroll limit 32, 14 rows to the next map, rows 2-15 shown: rows 14-15 are section 4's rows 0-1
$E69D Section4Map ; section 4: scroll limit 8, 7 rows to the next map, rows 2-9 shown: rows 7-9 are section 5's rows 0-2
$E6D5 Section5Map ; section 5: scroll limit 16, 11 rows to the next map, rows 2-11 shown: row 11 is section 6's row 0
$E72D Section6Map ; section 6: scroll limit 104, 32 rows to the next map, rows 2-33 shown: rows 32-33 are section 7's rows 0-1
$E82D Section7Map ; section 7: scroll limit 16, 10 rows shown up to its row 11
$E88D b MapsTail1 5 ; 317 bytes of older data (5-byte groups such as 00 00 FD 03 03), read by no code
> Not level 4's bytes, and no section shows them; their origin is not known. BuildView reads the first 24 bytes as the 3 rows it builds below the view of the section whose shown rows end at $E88D (scrolled to its limit); no section shows them.
$E9CA s MapsTail2 ; 35 zero bytes after the maps
> Zero bytes up to the next block.
$E9ED b MapsTail3 8 ; 768 bytes: level 4's bytes at the same addresses (an older build)
> Byte for byte the level 4 block at $E9ED-$ECEC: its last map rows ($E9ED-$EABC), the bytes after them, its section table ($EAED-$EB54) and the first of its lists. Level 5 was laid over a level 4 image; no code reads them (level 5's table is at $ECED).

@module sections ; The section table, the exit lists and the map entry lists
$ECED b SectionTable 8 ; 8 entries of 8 bytes: 8 sections
> LevelHeader+2 points here; SetupSection $8A6D reads entry A at SectionTable + 8A. +0/+1 the map's offset from Maps (big-endian); +2 0, read by no code; +3 the scroll limit, the last ViewTop (cells, a multiple of 8) the scroll down may reach ($88D2); +4/+5 the exit list's offset from SectionTable (big-endian), into CheckSectionExit's LD IX,n; +6/+7 the map entry list's offset from SectionTable (big-endian), into SectionEntries $8B3E. The number of entries is not stored: SectionCounts $8374 says 8 for this level (ResetSectionObjects $8379 walks that many lists).
@res level/sections.bin
$ECED ; section 0: map +$0000, limit 104, exits +$0040, entries +$0045
$ECF5 ; section 1: map +$0110, limit 8, exits +$0116, entries +$011B
$ECFD ; section 2: map +$0160, limit 80, exits +$0170, entries +$0179
$ED05 ; section 3: map +$0240, limit 32, exits +$0246, entries +$024B
$ED0D ; section 4: map +$02B0, limit 8, exits +$02DC, entries +$02E1
$ED15 ; section 5: map +$02E8, limit 16, exits +$0332, entries +$0337
$ED1D ; section 6: map +$0340, limit 104, exits +$0398, entries +$03A1
$ED25 ; section 7: map +$0440, limit 16, exits +$04CE, entries +$04D3
$ED2D b Section0Exits 4 ; Section 0's exits: 1, then the 0 end
> 4 bytes an exit: +0 flags (bits 0-1 the side Rick leaves the view at: 1 left, 2 right; bit 4 ends the level even with CheatExits; bit 5 RideMode on the way in; bit 6 LevelHookOn = 20; bit 7 the level ends), +1 the section row of Rick's feet (ViewTop + (y + 20) / 8) at which it is taken, +2 the section entered, +3 the row of that section his feet arrive at (the new ViewTop = +3 - his feet row in the view). A byte with bits 0, 1, 3 clear ends the list (a single 0). CheckSectionExit $8993 reads them.
@res level/sections.bin
$ED2D ; flags $02: leaves at the right at row $2B -> section 1, row $1B
$ED31 ; the end
$ED32 b Section0Objects 4 ; Section 0's map entries: 31, then the 0 end
> The map entries, sorted by row: 4 bytes each, then +3 bits 0-1 trigger boxes of 4 bytes. +0 the kind (bit 7 set while its object lives: ResetSectionObjects $8379 and FreeObject $9C57 clear it): below $74 a trap or pickup through KindTable, $78 the countdown bonus starts, $7C it stops and pays, any other value from $74 an enemy (bits 0-1 its behaviour: 0 and 3 wander, 1 patrols, 2 chases, 3 also climbs after Rick; bit 2 the second sprite set). +1 the cell row in the section. +2 bits 0-4 the column (x = column x 8), bit 5 x + 4 pixels, bit 6 y + 3 pixels (traps), bit 7 the spawn rule (set: when its row comes into the view; clear: when one of its boxes fires). +3 bits 0-1 the boxes, bits 2-5 a trap's behaviour ($20 treasure, $28 ammunition, $2C detonators: pickups) or an enemy's patrol length ((n + 1) x 8 pixels), bit 6 one-shot (never re-armed). A kind 0 ends the list.
> A trigger box: +0 x in pixels, +1 the cell row, +2 the size (high nibble rows - 1, low nibble columns - 1), +3 who sets it off (bit 0 Rick, 1 the bullet, 2 the blast, 3 the punch, 4 an enemy, 5 another trap; bit 6 fires on every pass; bit 7 the latch TestTriggerBoxes $A69F keeps). ScanMapObjects $8B42 and SpawnFromMap $9C97 read the entries, TestTriggerBoxes the boxes.
@res level/sections.bin
