@equ $7A97 PlayEffect ; the game's effect start (A = the effect)
@equ $7ED3 AddScore ; adds the six digits at HL to the score
@equ $7F05 Points5000 6 ; 005000: the Fat Guy's points
@equ $7B8F TrapRecords 145 ; the six 24-byte trap records (table A, $FF at $7C1F); the Fat Guy takes the first five
@equ $7C20 EnemyRecords 93 ; the four 23-byte enemy records (table B); the Fat Guy's shot uses the first
@equ $8A24 LevelHookOn 2 ; nonzero: the level's own code runs (20 at the Fat Guy's exit; his hits left)
@equ $8C8D BulletHits ; C when the bullet lies in the box E, HL, C x B
@equ $8CAE BlastHits ; C when the detonator's blast overlaps the box
@equ $8D0C ProbePoint ; what lies at E, HL: 2 for a solid object, else the cell's flags
@equ $8D53 TouchesRick ; C when the box E, HL, C x B overlaps Rick
@equ $93D0 KillRick ; Rick dies
@equ $9BAC RickX ; Rick's x
@equ $9BAD RickY 2 ; Rick's y (pixels from the view's top)
@equ $9BB4 BulletRecord ; +0: the bullet flies
@equ $9BC6 DetonatorFuse ; the detonator's fuse: the blast at 1-10

@isub $F8F3 LD HL,Sprites+43*128
@isub $F901 LD HL,Sprites+44*128
@isub $FA8F LD HL,Sprites+62*128
@isub $F993 LD HL,$FB00

@module header
$C206 c LevelHookA ; JP FatGuyStart: the Fat Guy's setup, from SetupSection
> Called by SetupSection ($8AF8) in place of ScanMapObjects when LevelHookOn is set: the section set up after the exit into section 7, and again after each of Rick's deaths there.
> Witness: L1 2 (rzx-l5-20000-poke).

$C209 c LevelHookB ; JP FatGuyUpdate: a pass of the Fat Guy, from UpdateObjects
> Called by UpdateObjects ($8664) every pass while LevelHookOn is set, in place of the trap and enemy updates (Rick, his bullet and his detonator are still updated after it).
> Witness: L1 524 (rzx-l5-20000-poke, rzx-l5-22000-poke).

@module fatguy ; Level 5's own code: the Fat Guy
@about Level 5 ends with the Fat Guy (section 7): its exit $42 from section 6 sets LevelHookOn $8A24 to 20, and while it is nonzero the game hands the section's objects to this code: SetupSection calls LevelHookA ($C206) in place of the map entry scan, UpdateObjects calls LevelHookB ($C209) in place of the trap and enemy updates. The Fat Guy is five trap records moved together along one path; he takes 20 hits of the bullet or the blast (LevelHookOn counts them; a death of Rick sets it back to 20, $93E2), shoots one shot at a time at Rick, and kills him by touch or by the shot. At the last hit he gives 5000 points and falls apart: the records get paths and blast animations that the normal trap code then plays, since LevelHookOn is 0 again.
@about Witnessed only in rzx-l5-20000-poke and rzx-l5-22000-poke (the Fat Guy's fight; the kill in the second).

$F7E6 c FatGuyStart ; Put the Fat Guy into the first five trap records
> For each of the five records of FatGuyParts (8 bytes each) the trap record TrapRecords + 24k: +0 = $11 (alive, behaviour $10), +6 = $60 (no trigger test, freed at the path's end), the step count +$0E = 0, +7 and +8 from the part's +0/+1, the sprite +4/+5, the path +$0F/+$10 and the animation +$0A/+$0B. Then the body (record 0) at x $B0, y $0090 with FatGuyPath as its path. The other records' paths and animations only play after the kill (UpdateTrap); until then FatGuyUpdate places them.
> Witness: L1 2 (rzx-l5-20000-poke).
$F7F2 FatGuyStartLoop ; one part: alive, behaviour $10, no trigger test
$F7FE ; +7 / +8: the size, for the parts 1-4 the offset from the body
$F808 ; +4/+5: the sprite
$F812 ; +$0F/+$10: the path played after the kill
$F81C ; +$0A/+$0B: the animation played after the kill
$F82A ; the body at x $B0, y $90
$F835 ; the body's path while he lives

$F83C b FatGuyParts 8 ; The Fat Guy's five records: x offset or width, y offset or height, sprite, path, animation
> 8 bytes a part, read by FatGuyStart: +0 the width (record +7), +1 the height (+8): for the parts 1-4 FatGuyPlacePart reads them as the offset from the body; +2/+3 the sprite address; +4/+5 the path after the kill; +6/+7 the animation after the kill.
@res level/fat-guy.bin
$F83C ; part 0, the body: size 0 x 0, sprite 45, path $F864, animation $F8C5
$F844 ; part 1: at x - 24, y + 21, sprite 40, path $F879, animation $F8C7
$F84C ; part 2: at x, y + 21, sprite 41, path $F88E, animation $F8C9
$F854 ; part 3: at x - 24, y + 42, sprite 42, path $F8A3, animation $F8CB
$F85C ; part 4: at x, y + 42, sprite 44 (43 and 44 every other pass while he lives), path $F8B4, animation $F8C5

$F864 b FatGuyPiecePaths 8 ; The paths the five parts fly off on after the kill (the trap path format)
> Count and step pairs, then 0 and the loop address. Each piece jumps (y steps -8 to -2), then falls 8 pixels a pass; parts 1 and 3 drift left, 0, 2 and 4 right, until UpdateTrap frees them off the view or at the loop point (+6 bit 5).
@res level/fat-guy.bin
$F864 ; part 0
$F879 ; part 1
$F88E ; part 2
$F8A3 ; part 3
$F8B4 ; part 4

$F8C5 b FatGuyPieceAnims 8 ; The pieces' animation: frames $22-$25 (sprites 20-23, the blast), looped
> One script entered at four places (parts 0 and 4 at $F8C5, 1 at $F8C7, 2 at $F8C9, 3 at $F8CB): frames $22, $22, $23, $23, $24, $24, $25, $25, then $FF and the jump back to $F8C5.
@res level/fat-guy.bin

$F8D0 c FatGuyUpdate ; A pass of the Fat Guy: move, place the parts, the hits, the touch, the shot
> The body (TrapRecords) one step along its path (FatGuyMove), the four other parts placed at their offsets from it (FatGuyPlacePart), part 4's sprite switched between 43 and 44 every pass (its +9 toggled). The box for the hits is part 1's x and y, 48 x 42 (the four lower parts): the bullet in it (BulletHits) is spent and counts a hit; the detonator's blast at fuse 4-9 (BlastHits) counts a hit on every such pass (FatGuyHit). Then the same box against Rick (TouchesRick): JP KillRick. Then the shot: FatGuyShoot, FatGuyShotMove with IX = EnemyRecords.
> Witness: 524 runs (rzx-l5-20000-poke 377, rzx-l5-22000-poke 147); hits 14, all from the blast ($F940; the bullet branch $F922 never ran); the touch never killed (the JP C at $F946 never jumped).
> Witness: L1 524 (rzx-l5-20000-poke, rzx-l5-22000-poke).
$F8F3 ; part 4's sprite: 43 or 44 by its +9
$F904 FatGuyLegsStore
$F907 ; the hit box: part 1's y and x, 48 wide, 42 high
$F929 FatGuyBlastTest ; the blast hurts at fuse 4-9
$F943 FatGuyTouchTest ; the same box against Rick
$F949 ; the shot

$F954 c FatGuyHit ; A hit: one off LevelHookOn; at 0 the Fat Guy dies
> Effect 9; LevelHookOn - 1. Not 0: return. 0: 5000 points (AddScore with Points5000), effects 5 and 10; the body's path becomes FatGuyPiecePaths and its animation FatGuyPieceAnims (the other parts already hold theirs); the enemy record EnemyRecords takes the body's x and y as a dying enemy (+$14 = $FF, +0 = 1, the vertical speed +$0E/+$0F = $FB00, -5.0: KillEnemy's jump). With LevelHookOn 0 the next pass of UpdateObjects runs the trap and enemy updates again: the five parts fly off and blow up, the enemy record falls.
> Witness: 14 runs (rzx-l5-20000-poke 6, rzx-l5-22000-poke 8); the kill once ($F961, rzx-l5-22000-poke).
> Witness: L1 14 (rzx-l5-20000-poke, rzx-l5-22000-poke).
$F961 ; LevelHookOn reached 0: the Fat Guy dies
$F971 ; the pieces' path and animation for the body
$F97D ; a dying enemy at the body's place
$F993 ; -5.0 in 8.8: the dying jump

$F99A c FatGuyMove ; One step of the trap record at IX along its path (no effects, no view test)
> UpdateTrap's path step without the $FF effects, the off-view tests and the waits: +$0E counts the passes of the current pair (count, step); when it reaches the count the next pair is taken, and a 0 count jumps to the address after it. The step: the high nibble added to x, the low nibble (signed) to the 16-bit y, 7 meaning 8. Only the body (TrapRecords) is moved so.
> Witness: L1 524 (rzx-l5-20000-poke, rzx-l5-22000-poke).
$F9A7 ; the pair is done: the next one
$F9B3 FatGuyPathStore
$F9BA FatGuyPathStep ; one more pass of this pair
$F9BF ; the x step: the high nibble, 7 meaning 8
$F9CE FatGuyStoreX
$F9D8 ; the y step: the low nibble, signed
$F9E3 FatGuyStepY
$F9E9 FatGuyStoreY

$F9F8 b FatGuyShotDelay 1 ; Passes before the Fat Guy may shoot again (50 after a shot)
> Set to 50 by FatGuyShoot at each shot, counted down by it; 0 in the image.

$F9F9 c FatGuyShoot ; Start a shot at Rick when the delay is over and no shot flies
> FatGuyShotDelay counts down to 0 first. Then, when EnemyRecords +0 is 0 (no shot): the delay = 50, the record on (+0 = 1), the shot at x = the body's x - 24, y = the body's y + 52 (8 bits; +3 = 0). The aim: B = Rick's y + 10 - the shot's x, C = Rick's x + 12 - the shot's y (as written, x and y are crossed: with the Fat Guy on the right and Rick level with him the shot still goes left at Rick; to check on screen); both are halved together (SRA) until C is within 1-3 (or -4 to -1) and B within -4 to 3. A difference past -128 is halved once and made negative first ($FA3D).
> Witness: 525 runs; 11 shots ($FA05); the branch for C positive ($FA46, Rick to the right of the shot) never ran.
> Witness: L1 525 (rzx-l5-20000-poke, rzx-l5-22000-poke).
$FA05 FatGuyShotNew ; no delay left: is a shot flying?
$FA17 ; y = the body's y + 52, x = the body's x - 24
$FA29 ; B: Rick's y + 10 - the shot's x
$FA30 ; C: Rick's x + 12 - the shot's y
$FA3D ; a borrow past -128: halve once, keep it negative
$FA46 AimRightLoop ; C positive: halve until C < 4 and B in -4..3 (not executed in any recording)
$FA59 AimRightDownTest
$FA5E AimRightHalve
$FA65 AimLeftLoop ; C negative: halve until C >= -4 and B in -4..3
$FA78 AimLeftDownTest
$FA7D AimLeftHalve

$FA84 b FatGuyShootLeftover 3 ; 01 F8 00: LD BC,$00F8, not executed in any recording (no jump reaches it)
> Three bytes between the halving loop's JP and FatGuyShotAimed: an instruction of an older build.

$FA87 c FatGuyShotAimed ; FatGuyShoot, continued: the steps and the shot's sprite
> The x step C into EnemyRecords +7, the y step B into +8, the sprite 62 into +4/+5.
> Witness: 11 runs (rzx-l5-20000-poke 8, rzx-l5-22000-poke 3).
> Witness: L1 11 (rzx-l5-20000-poke, rzx-l5-22000-poke).

$FA96 c FatGuyShotMove ; Move the shot in the record at IX (EnemyRecords): Rick, the walls, the edge
> Nothing when its +0 is 0. x += +7: from $F8 on the shot ends (+0 = 0). y (the low byte at +2 only) += +8. Then a 1 x 1 box at x + 4, y + 4 against Rick (TouchesRick: KillRick, the one recorded death by $FACB) and the point's flags (ProbePoint): bit 1 (a wall or a solid object) ends the shot.
> Witness: 525 runs; the shot moved 335 passes, left the view 2 times ($FAA6), hit a wall 7 times ($FAD7); KillRick from $FACB once (rzx-l5-20000-poke or -22000-poke, the caller list of $93D0).
> Witness: L1 525 (rzx-l5-20000-poke, rzx-l5-22000-poke).
$FAAB FatGuyShotOn
$FABB ; the point x + 4, y + 4: a 1 x 1 box against Rick
$FAD0 ; a wall at the point ends the shot

$FADB c FatGuyPlacePart ; Put the part at IX at its offset (+7, +8) from the body
> x = the body's x + (IX+7) (8 bits), y = the body's y + (IX+8). Called four times a pass by FatGuyUpdate (parts 1-4).
> Witness: 2099 runs from each of $F8DB, $F8E2, $F8E9, $F8F0.
> Witness: L1 2099 (rzx-l5-20000-poke, rzx-l5-22000-poke).

$FAF4 b FatGuyPath 8 ; The Fat Guy's path while he lives (FatGuyMove's format)
> Count and step pairs: 24 passes still, small steps up and right ($1F, $2E, $1D, $1C, $0C), back ($FC, $FD, $EE, $FF), 24 still, a loop down and left and back ($F0 ... $10), then 0 and the jump to FatGuyPath ($FB30).
@res level/fat-guy-path.bin
$FB30 ; 0: the loop back to FatGuyPath

$FB33 s FreeTail ; 461 zero bytes to the block's end $FCFF
> Read by no code.
